  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 454 AM EDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nThe extended periods begin Tue night with a large high pressure \nridge centered over western NC with its ridge axis extending from \nthe Mid-Atlantic down to the MS/AL Gulf coast. This ridge stays \nfairly stationary through Thu keeping things warm and dry but it \ndoes weaken slowly through the end of the week. Temperatures Wed, \nThu, and Fri will be in the 80s to near 90s with lows mainly in the \n60s. As this ridge begins to weaken Thu,  a fairly organized frontal \nboundary begins to move SE out of the upper to middle MS river \nvalley. It makes its way SE Thu night to just NW of the AL/GA/TN \nborder by daybreak Fri. As this front moves SE both the ECMWF and \nthe GFS show it loosing strength as a lot of its dynamics and \nmoisture moving NE closer to the parent low center which will be \nover the New England states by this time. Just the southern tail end \nof this front swings SE through the state Fri/Fri night with the \nmodels showing it moving into N FL by daybreak Sat. If this holds \ntrue all we should see from this frontal passage is maybe some \nincreased cloud cover but the biggest things we will see is very \nFall like temps moving into the area next weekend.\n\nThe models show a very strong high pressure ridge pushing into the \narea from the northern/central plains behind the exiting frontal \nboundary Fri. This ridge will bring a cooler/drier airmass with it \ncooling the Southeastern U.S. off substantially. We could see highs \ntemps in the 60s and 70s Sat with lows in the 40s Sat night. May \neven see some upper 30s across the NE GA mountains. Sundays High \ntemps expected to be in the 60s. We could literally go from wearing \nshorts and short sleeves Fri...to sweaters and jackets Sun morning.",
